That works well. A `/route` slash command arrives as an interaction POST, so you query D1 for the next upcoming route and return it as an embed with an RSVP button. Something like:

ts
async function handleRoute(env: Env) {
  const route = await env.DB
    .prepare('SELECT * FROM routes WHERE run_date >= date(\'now\') ORDER BY run_date LIMIT 1')
    .first<Route>();

  if (!route) {
    return json({ type: 4, data: { content: 'No route posted yet.', flags: 64 } });
  }

  return json({
    type: 4,
    data: {
      embeds: [{
        title: route.name,
        description: `${route.distance_km} km on ${route.run_date}`,
        url: route.map_url,
      }],
      components: [{
        type: 1,
        components: [{ type: 2, style: 3, label: 'RSVP', custom_id: `rsvp:${route.id}` }],
      }],
    },
  });
}

Run a one-off script that PUTs the command definition to Discord:

ts
await fetch(`https://discord.com/api/v10/applications/${APP_ID}/commands`, {
  method: 'PUT',
  headers: { Authorization: `Bot ${BOT_TOKEN}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify([{ name: 'route', description: 'Post this week\'s running club route' }]),
});


PUT overwrites the whole command list, so include any existing commands in the array or they'll be removed.

Here's a sketch using discord.js v14 slash commands:

ts
import { SlashCommandBuilder, ChatInputCommandInteraction, EmbedBuilder } from 'discord.js';

export const data = new SlashCommandBuilder()
  .setName('gallery')
  .setDescription('Share photos from a recent run')
  .addStringOption(o => o.setName('run').setDescription('Which run (date or route name)').setRequired(true))
  .addAttachmentOption(o => o.setName('photo1').setDescription('First photo').setRequired(true))
  .addAttachmentOption(o => o.setName('photo2').setDescription('Second photo'))
  .addAttachmentOption(o => o.setName('photo3').setDescription('Third photo'));

export async function execute(interaction: ChatInputCommandInteraction) {
  const run = interaction.options.getString('run', true);
  const photos = ['photo1', 'photo2', 'photo3']
    .map(n => interaction.options.getAttachment(n))
    .filter((a): a is NonNullable<typeof a> => a !== null);

  const embeds = photos.map((p, i) =>
    new EmbedBuilder()
      .setTitle(i === 0 ? `Photos from ${run}` : '')
      .setImage(p.url)
  );

  await interaction.reply({ embeds });
}


Discord caps slash commands at 25 options and messages at 10 embeds, so if you want bigger galleries later, a follow-up upload flow or a thread per run might work better. Want me to add an RSVP-aware check so only people who RSVP'd can post?

Here's a simple version. I'm assuming you have some way to look up RSVPs by run, so I've stubbed it as `getRsvps(run)`. Swap in whatever your RSVP storage actually uses.

ts
import { getRsvps } from '../rsvp'; // adjust to your actual module

export async function execute(interaction: ChatInputCommandInteraction) {
  const run = interaction.options.getString('run', true);

  const rsvps = await getRsvps(run); // string[] of Discord user IDs
  if (!rsvps.includes(interaction.user.id)) {
    await interaction.reply({
      content: `Only people who RSVP'd to ${run} can post photos.`,
      ephemeral: true,
    });
    return;
  }

  // ...build embeds and reply as before
}


The ephemeral reply keeps the rejection private so nobody gets called out in the channel. When you're in the Pacebot repo, I can wire this to the real RSVP code instead of the stub.
